{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the angel of YHWH appeared to him in a flame of fire</b>. Scripture first says <b>and the angel of YHWH appeared</b>, and afterward (Exodus 3:4) it says <b>and YHWH saw that he turned aside to see, and Elohim called to him</b>. Therefore Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 3:6, short commentary) said that \"Elohim\" here is the aforementioned angel, as in \"for I have seen Elohim face to face\" (Genesis 32:31), and the meaning of \"I am the Elohim of your father\" (Exodus 3:6) is that the emissary speaks in the language of the one who sent him. But this is not correct, for Moses, who was at a great level of prophecy, would not have hidden his face from an angel. And our Rabbis said in Bereshit Rabbah (97:4, and cf. Shemot Rabbah 2:8): this angel was Michael. Rabbi Yosi haAruch said: everywhere they saw him they would say there, \"our holy master\"; so too everywhere Michael appears, there is the glory of the Shekhinah. Their intention is to say that at first Michael appeared to him, and there was the glory of the Shekhinah, but he did not see the glory because he had not prepared his mind for prophecy, and when he directed his heart and turned aside to see, the vision of the Shekhinah was revealed to him and Elohim called to him from the midst of the bush.\nAnd by way of truth, this angel is the Angel the Redeemer, of whom it is said \"for My name is in him\" (Exodus 23:21). He it is who said to Jacob \"I am the El of Beth-el\" (Genesis 31:13), and of him it is written \"and He called him Elohim,\" but in that attribute he is called an angel in the governance of the world. And thus it is written \"and YHWH brought us out of Egypt\" (Deuteronomy 26:8) and it is written \"and He sent an angel and brought us out of Egypt\" (Numbers 20:16), and it is said \"and the angel of His presence saved them\" (Isaiah 63:9), meaning an angel who is His presence, as it is written \"My presence shall go and I will give you rest\" (Exodus 33:14). And it is said of him \"and suddenly HaShem whom you seek will come to His temple, and the angel of the covenant whom you desire, behold he comes\" (Malachi 3:1). And you will further understand this from the verses that follow (Exodus 9:12, 13, 15), with the help of the Name:" "<b>and behold, the bush was burning in fire</b> — meaning kindled, that it was within burning fire, as in \"and the fathers kindle the fire\" (Jeremiah 7:18) — they light it, \"and they shall kindle a fire in them\" (Ezekiel 39:9). And <b>why does the bush not burn</b> — it is not consumed and eaten, and like it \"like flax that was burned in fire\" (Judges 15:14) — was consumed. And so is the opinion of Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 3:2), who translated the first instance as בָּעַר (burning) and the second as מִתּוֹקַד (being consumed). Or <b>יִבְעַר</b> may be from the root of \"and you shall burn (וּבִעַרְתָּ) the evil from your midst\" (Deuteronomy 17:7), \"and it shall be for a man to burn (לְבָעֵר)\" (Isaiah 44:15), for it is a way of elegance in the sacred tongue to speak thus, \"riding on thirty donkeys (עֲיָרִים) and thirty donkeys (עֲיָרִים) for them\" (Judges 10:4):"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :register {:fixes 1}, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Ibn Ezra by his epithet 'Rabbi Abraham'; the reference is to Ibn Ezra's short commentary on Exodus 3:6."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:01:35.871423511Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}